$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The dispute in outline</h2>
<p>Saha Infratech Pvt. Ltd., a real estate developer, raised money through non-convertible debentures held entirely by ECL Finance Ltd., which separately extended a term loan. Palm Developers Pvt. Ltd. stood as corporate guarantor, and IDBI Trusteeship Services Ltd. acted as debenture trustee. When insolvency proceedings commenced against the guarantor in January 2020, the trustee and ECL together claimed admission to the Committee of Creditors for debts aggregating some ₹270 crore, and were initially admitted.</p>
<p>A homebuyer objected: the lender's contractual grip over the debtor group, he argued, made it a related party, disqualified from the CoC. The NCLT agreed, the NCLAT affirmed, and on 27 October 2026 the Supreme Court dismissed the appeals.</p>

<h2>Participation in policy, not control</h2>
<p>The heart of the judgment is the construction of Section 5(24)(m)(i) IBC, which treats as a related party a person who participates in the policy-making process of the corporate debtor. The appellants pressed for a demanding reading aligned with "control" as understood under Section 29A(c) and (g) and explained in <em>ArcelorMittal India Pvt. Ltd. v. Satish Kumar Gupta</em>. The Court refused the assimilation. The related-party definition, it held, requires only an association with the corporate debtor by way of participation in its policy-making; a creditor need not command the enterprise. In the Court's words, a role of navigation — determining policy and channelising business interests — suffices.</p>
<div class="check">
<p>The documents did the rest. Reading the articles of association, debenture trust deed, facility agreement and sanction letter together, the Court found rights over approval of business plans, nominee directors with deadlock-breaking authority, managerial appointments, inspection and observer rights, control of project construction and sales through a power of attorney, and revenues routed through escrow. Taken cumulatively, this was overall supervision of the debtor's affairs, not the ordinary negative covenants of a secured lender.</p>
</div>

<h2>The exclusion and its reach</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>The statutory bar.</strong> The first proviso to Section 21(2) IBC strips a related-party financial creditor of representation, participation and voting in the CoC. Once ECL was found to be a related party, its exclusion followed in both its capacities — debenture holder and term lender.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The trustee falls with the holder.</strong> The debenture trustee exists to protect the debenture holder's interests. Where the sole holder is excluded as a related party, the Court held, the trustee cannot re-enter the CoC in its own name and vote the same economic interest.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Assignment scrutinised.</strong> ECL's assignment of its facility-agreement rights after commencement of the insolvency process was found not to be bona fide — a device to escape the proviso — and therefore ineffective to launder the disqualification.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Homebuyer heard.</strong> The Court also affirmed that an individual homebuyer may object to the inclusion of alleged related parties; the mechanism of the authorised representative for a creditor class does not silence individual financial creditors on such questions.</div>
</div>

<h2>Guarantees as financial debt</h2>
<p>One strand of the judgment favours lenders. The tribunals below had doubted whether a corporate guarantee not invoked before insolvency commencement could found a financial debt. The Supreme Court disagreed: financial debt under Section 5(8) does not presuppose default or invocation, a position consistent with its earlier jurisprudence on guarantees. The appellants lost not because their claim was bad in character, but because their relationship with the debtor group was too close for the CoC.</p>

<h2>What transactional and insolvency lawyers should take away</h2>
<p>The judgment forces a trade-off into the open. Covenant-heavy documentation protects a lender during the life of the loan, but the same clauses can be read back, cumulatively, as participation in policy once insolvency supervenes — with loss of the CoC seat as the price. Facilities should be documented, and nominee rights exercised, with Section 5(24)(m)(i) in view; protective covenants confined to genuine lender protection stand a better chance than rights that reach into business planning, sales and management. On the contest side, resolution professionals, creditors and homebuyers examining a CoC's composition now have a clear instruction: assess the documents as a whole for a navigation role, and treat post-commencement assignments of disqualified debt with suspicion.</p>
<p>For a Delhi practice appearing before the NCLT and NCLAT, the decision will be the starting point of every related-party objection argued hereafter.</p>
